THE DOOR READ, 2026-08-22 (Clay dispatch amendment 11, box one)

THE QUESTION: station 6's harvest fires the commissioned artifact and loses
its tail. Containment covers parseRule's fire site. Which door is this?

THE ANSWER, and it is OUTSIDE the enumerated three:

    A about to <- the BlocK off the twin
    B <- done, no fire yet; about to TRUTH-TEST it with if
    987654 C if done, doArt 1        <- FIRED HERE, and the run ends

The door is `if doArt;` -- aCTionIF's condition fork at GroupRules.mm:857,
`if (isMethod(instructType)) result = gMethod(result)`. A BlocK carries
aCTionBlocK as its gMethod, so truth-testing a BlocK EXECUTES THE BODY.

It is NOT fireLabelMethod, NOT parseSetLabel, NOT actK. Those three are the
rule-action doors. This is the ordinary `if` condition evaluation, and it can
fire ANY node carrying a gMethod -- which after commissioning includes both
the rule and its BlocK.

⚠ THE POSITIVE CONTROL IS WHY THIS READS AT ALL. The first bisect ran clean
end to end and "proved" station 6 does not fire -- because compile had left no
BlocK and steps 1 to 3 were operating on nothing. A bisect that never armed
its subject proves nothing, and it looked exactly like a result. The control
(`SETUP twin carries a BlocK 1`) fires the marker itself, which is how the
truncation became visible.

⚠ THIS IS TWO TRAPS MEETING ON ONE LINE. Bear-trap #34 says truth-testing a
value that carries a gMethod fires it; #37 says a fired body's return unwinds
the block. `if frArt;` does both: it fires the artifact and then loses the rest
of the station. The frontier's own prose already forbids truth-testing
rule-shaped values -- the rule simply was not extended to BlocK-shaped ones,
because nobody had commissioned an artifact when it was written.

WHAT THIS MEANS FOR CONTAINMENT: applying yield-consumption at this door is a
DIFFERENT decision from applying it at parseRule's. parseRule fires a body
deliberately, as the rule's parse implementation, and consuming its return is
plainly right. An `if` condition firing a method is ordinary language
behaviour on ordinary traffic -- consuming isBranch there changes what `if`
means for every method-valued condition in the language, not just for
commissioned artifacts. That is a ruling, not an edit.

THE CHEAPER ROUTE, offered not taken: the frontier need not truth-test the
BlocK at all. Its own standing discipline says assert on artifacts a fired
action cannot fake -- list length, or presence by subscript without an `if`.
Extending that discipline from rule-shaped to method-shaped nodes closes the
door in the instrument without touching the language.
